<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Stand Up! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> It's a School Day!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Stand Up!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b><br>
            Reading: reading_1: Recognize basic familiar words supported with illustrations or visual aids.<br>
            reading_2: Attempt to read basic and familiar words supported with pictures.<br>
            phonemic_awareness: Identify sounds at the beginning of words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b><br>
            Reading: Can recognize familiar words about classroom language supported with pictures (e.g., the word 'window' supported with a picture).<br>
            Can attempt to read basic familiar words with picture support during chants, rhymes, or shared reading (e.g., 'cat' with a picture of a cat).<br>
            Can identify the initial sound of a familiar word when heard, with the support of a picture (e.g., sound /b/ with the word 'book' supported with a picture of a book).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e0e6ea;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 17%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the theme and initial vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Good Morning Song and Action" using picture flashcards for 'morning', 'teacher', 'student'. Students greet the teacher and each other, responding to simple instructions with actions (TPR). The teacher displays large flashcards with words like 'door', 'window', 'chair', 'desk' on the board. The teacher calls out a word (e.g., "door!") and points to the corresponding word flashcard. Students point to the object in the classroom and the word on the board. The teacher says, "Please, stand up!" and students stand up. The teacher points to the word "stand up" on a flashcard.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models classroom language using imperative sentences and picture flashcards. For example, "Please, <b>stand up!</b>" (teacher stands up and points to the word 'stand up'). "Please, <b>listen</b>." (teacher cups ear and points to the word 'listen', emphasizing the /l/ sound). The teacher demonstrates how to identify words by pointing to the written word while saying it and showing the corresponding object/picture. The teacher models the initial sound for 'book' by saying "/b/, /b/, book!" while showing a book and its word.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the words and actions.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the 'window' flashcard and the actual window) "Is this a door or a window?" (Expected: "Window!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Performing "Stand up" action) "What am I doing? Sit down or stand up?" (Expected: "Stand up!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's a School Day!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, illustrated "Our School Day" big book or interactive story on a projector. The story features simple sentences, large clear print, and vivid pictures for vocabulary such as 'school' (emphasizing /s/ sound), 'book', 'teacher' (syllable stress), 'student' (syllable stress), 'chair', 'desk', 'table', 'window', 'door', 'music', 'listen'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will recognize basic familiar words about classroom language supported with illustrations and attempt to read them during a shared reading activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to each word as they read, and emphasizing target vocabulary and initial sounds. Students follow along, looking at the pictures and the words.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is this story about? Is it about a school or a park?" (Teacher points to 'school' word and picture).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Word Recognition:</b> "Show me the word 'book'!" (Teacher encourages students to point to the word 'book' in the big book). "Where is the 'teacher' in the picture? Can you find the word 'teacher'?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (with visuals):</b> (Teacher reads "It is a nice chair." and points to a picture of a chair.) "True or false? Is it a chair?" (Expected: "True!"). (Teacher points to 'window' word) "Is this a door?" (Expected: "False!").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice recognizing and attempting to read familiar words (chair, desk, table, book, window, door, teacher, student, listen, stand up, touch, open) with picture support and identifying initial sounds (/b/, /l/, /t/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Picture-Word Match" (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides each student with a worksheet containing pictures of classroom objects (e.g., a book, a chair, a window) and a word bank with the corresponding words (book, chair, window, door, desk). Students draw a line from each picture to its correct word. The teacher circulates, offering help and reinforcing word recognition. The teacher asks, "What is this word?" pointing to 'book', then "What sound does 'book' start with? /b/, /b/, book!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Read and Act" (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> The teacher displays flashcards with simple imperative sentences and pictures (e.g., "Please, <b>stand up!</b>" with a stick figure standing; "<b>Touch</b> the <b>door!</b>" with a stick figure touching a door). The teacher reads the sentence aloud, pointing to each word. Students listen and then perform the action. Then, the teacher holds up a flashcard with only the written word (e.g., 'jump') and students attempt to read it with support from a previously shown picture, performing the action if they recognize it. Focus on identifying words 'touch' (initial /t/ sound) and 'listen' (initial /l/ sound).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Word Hunt" (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a large picture of a classroom scene with 5-6 labeled objects (e.g., 'desk', 'chair', 'table', 'window', 'book'). One student reads a word from a list provided by the teacher (e.g., "table"). The other student finds the word on the classroom scene picture, points to it, and says the word aloud. They take turns. The teacher monitors, ensuring students attempt to read the words and provide peer feedback for recognition.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's a School Day!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Classroom Labeling" and "Show & Tell." Students will work in small groups to read and apply labels to real objects in the classroom, then present their work.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher prepares small cards with the target words ('chair', 'desk', 'table', 'window', 'door', 'book') and corresponding pictures.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups. Each group receives a set of word/picture cards.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The task is for each group to "read" the word on the card (with picture support), find the matching object in the classroom, and use tape to 'label' the object with the card. For example, one child picks up the 'door' card, attempts to read it, and then places it on the classroom door.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once all objects are labeled, each group takes turns doing a "Show & Tell." A student from the group points to one of their labeled objects (e.g., the 'window'), points to the word card, and attempts to read the word: "This is a window." or simply "Window!" (using the present simple and WH-question context if prompted by the teacher: "What is it?"). The teacher encourages them to use polite language like "Please look at the window."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher ensures all students participate, encouraging them to identify the initial sound of the word they are labeling or showing (e.g., "/w/, /w/, window!").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Classroom Labeling" and "Show & Tell" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will individually or in small groups point to a word label in the classroom (e.g., 'book') and attempt to read it. They will also respond to a teacher prompt about an initial sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Recognizes familiar words (with pictures):</b> Can the student correctly match and "read" the word labels (e.g., 'chair', 'book') to the objects? (Learning Outcome: recognizes familiar words).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Attempts to read:</b> Does the student try to vocalize the word, even if pronunciation is approximate? (Learning Outcome: attempt to read).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identifies initial sounds:</b> Can the student identify the initial sound of a word when prompted (e.g., "What sound does 'table' start with?" /t/)? (Learning Outcome: identify initial sound).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participates actively in group tasks.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a flashcard with a picture of a 'book' and the word 'book'. "Did you recognize the word 'book' today? Thumbs up if yes!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the first sound you hear in 'listen'? /l/, /l/, listen." (Students repeat the sound).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and planning for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you see and read today?" (Students may point to flashcards or objects).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite part of learning about our school day words?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students recognize and attempt to read words? (e.g., the visual support, TPR actions, repetition).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., more practice with specific initial sounds, providing clearer tracing options for reluctant readers, ensuring all students get a chance to 'read' aloud).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This reading lesson helped students recognize familiar words. In the next lesson (Speaking), students will use these words actively to describe actions and objects in the classroom, building on their word recognition to produce oral sentences and follow instructions, applying the grammar structures like imperatives and present simple.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>